  {
    "episode_id": 49034,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49034.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Certainly. So then let's fast forward then to now. A lot of this started with the AATIP. I think that there was some big significance that strum from it. More recently, the DOD has announced that it's formed a UAP task force that's working with the intelligence community, FAA, and other entities to really understand sort of this unexplained phenomena. At the request of the modern Congress, those officials released a short unclassified version of a larger intelligence report they submitted to lawmakers this summer. That report confirmed more than 100 reports originating from government sources. 80 of which involved observations with multiple sensors. But it also noted that data and collection capabilities were limited. Talk to me about some of the takeaways from this report and really thinking about how the collection capabilities that we have maybe have changed since the early 2000s with the AATIP to now. What was your takeaways and how does that all fit in?",
